In this lesson we'll look at what a logarithm actually is. Think of a logarithm as the opposite of raising a number to a power. If exponentiation asks, "what number do I get when I raise this base to some power," then a logarithm turns it around and asks, "what power did I need?" So the log of x in base a simply means the exponent you raise a by to land on x. For example, log base two of thirty-two equals five, because two raised to the fifth gives thirty-two. And log base ten of one thousand equals three, since ten cubed is one thousand. There's also a special version called the natural log, written "ln," which is just log base e, where e is a famous number about equal to two point seven one eight. It shows up a lot in science. Now, a couple of rules worth remembering. The input, x, always has to be positive, since you can only raise a number to a power and get something greater than zero. But the answer itself, the logarithm, can be any real number. And two quick ones: the log of one is always zero, and the log of the base itself is always one. Now let's see it in action.